anna: FYI folks, a customer reported an obscure crash only in Cloudflare Workers: "TypeError: response.body.getReader is not a function" when using our JS SDK. They bundled the SDK with a fetch polyfill for Node.

prashant: Sounds like a mismatch between fetch implementations again. Workers' Response uses a web-streams body; some polyfills provide a different body shape (no getReader). If the bundle puts a polyfill in, the SDK ends up talking to the wrong Response prototype.

olivia: Two practical fixes: 1) ensure no global polyfill for edge (don’t import any polyfill-globals in edge builds); 2) make the SDK tolerant by feature-detecting stream support instead of instanceof checks. Example detection we use in the SDK init:

```js
function hasWebStreams(res) {
  try {
    return !!(res && typeof res.body?.getReader === 'function');
  } catch (e) {
    return false;
  }
}

export async function preferredFetch(cliProvided) {
  if (cliProvided) return cliProvided;
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  try {
    const mod = await import('undici');
    return mod.fetch;
  } catch (e) {
    throw new Error('No fetch available - pass one via options');
  }
}
```

ben: For TypeScript we had a lot of confusion when projects included both lib.dom and undici types. We settled on exporting a minimal function type in our public API so consumers don't get type bleed: `export type SimpleFetch = (input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>;`. That keeps our d.ts small and generic.

anna: Should we add a compatibility note for bundlers like webpack/vite/rollup? The customer was using an old plugin that blindly injects a fetch shim.

prashant: Yes. Add a short checklist in README and sample-apps: 
- For Edge/Workers: do not add node fetch polyfills.
- For Node: prefer node 18+ or undici; let the SDK auto-detect via preferredFetch.
- For bundlers: ensure the plugin is disabled for edge bundles or add an alias to the native fetch.

olivia: Also document why we avoid global polyfills: constructor identity mismatches, streaming differences, and TypeScript troubleshooting (duplicate Request types). Quick FAQ blurb: "If you see streaming errors (getReader missing) or TS conflicts, remove polyfill-globals and pass a fetch into the client or rely on preferredFetch."

ben: Small DX idea: ship a tiny opt-in module `@redwood-inference/fetch-adapter` that exports `getFetch()` and type-safe `SimpleFetch` so sample-apps and customers can copy-paste one-liner usage. Could help reduce onboarding friction.

anna: Love that. I'll draft a tiny adapter and a one-file example for Next.js Edge + CJS consumer. Will open PR.

